### Validation, queries and endpoint

The graph is validated with SHACL {{ cite("w3c_shacl") }} using pySHACL {{ cite("sw_pyshacl") }}.
There are {{ f.shacl.shapes }} node shapes for cardinalities, datatypes, identifier formats and
provenance. SHACL-SPARQL constraints cover rules that a property path cannot express, such as
one current identification per organism, or a mapping record that must describe a SKOS triple
asserted in the graph. Violations stop the pipeline; warnings mirror the data-quality warnings.

There are {{ f.q.n_sparql | words }} SPARQL queries {{ cite("w3c_sparql") }} over the graph and
{{ f.q.n_sql | words }} SQL queries over the canonical tables in DuckDB {{ cite("raasveldt2019") }}.
Each SQL query must return exactly the rows of its SPARQL counterpart. The graph was also loaded
into an Oxigraph SPARQL endpoint {{ cite("sw_oxigraph") }} through the SPARQL 1.1 Graph Store
Protocol. Every query was run through the SPARQL 1.1 Protocol and compared with the in-process
results, which shows the queries are portable rather than specific to one engine.

### Reproducibility

The pipeline is a Python {{ f.versions.python }} package with a locked environment. It runs as
seven stages (Fig. 1), each logging its record counts in and out.

Continuous integration on every pull request runs:

- linting and the test suite;
- the pipeline offline, from the cached API responses;
- a check that every regenerated output is identical to the committed one;
- a Docker build that reruns the pipeline and the endpoint check.

An end-to-end test does the same locally into an empty directory. Development used one feature
branch and one reviewed pull request per stage.

## Results

### Extraction and harmonisation

All parts of the article listed in Table 1 were extracted. The row counts match the numbers the
article states: {{ s.t2 }} collected and {{ s.t3 }} examined specimens. Harmonisation produced
the canonical tables in Table 2. No record was lost: every specimen became one occurrence, and
every Table 1 code became one assertion. The only exception is the {{ c.n_folded | words }} codes for
new records ("N"), which were merged into the survey assertion they qualify.

