Working through the article cell by cell brought to light problems that a reader would hardly
notice:

- {{ c.typos | words }} misspelled names in Table 2;
- an impossible date in Table 3 ("31/04/2012");
- {{ s.t3_missing_state | words }} missing state and {{ d.by_id.W02 | words }} missing localities;
- {{ d.by_id.W03 | words }} museum specimens whose identification "could not be corroborated";
- the same order named Zoanthidea in Table 1 and Zoantharia in the species accounts;
- *Zoanthus pulchellus* in Table 1 but *Z.* cf. *pulchellus* in its species account and in
  Table 2.

Coordinates exist only for the {{ c.sites_with_coords }} collecting sites of Table 2, so no
locality was geocoded. Collection dates were stated in the Methods of the article only for
four of the sampled areas, so {{ c.t2_dated }} of the {{ s.t2 }} collected specimens carry a
date. The remainder were left undated rather than assumed: the Isla de Cubagua material, for
instance, was collected over several years (information from the present author, a co-author of the
article), so no single date describes it.

### Identifier resolution

WoRMS resolved all {{ r.worms.accepted }} names, GBIF {{ r.gbif.accepted }} and NCBI Taxonomy
{{ r.ncbi.accepted }} (Fig. 3, Table 3). WoRMS held more than one record for
{{ r.homonyms_total | words }} of the names. For {{ r.homonyms_by_authority | words }} of them, the authority
printed in the article picked the right record. For example, *Zoanthus sociatus* (Ellis, 1768)
was matched to the accepted record, not to a *taxon inquirendum* of the same name by Le Sueur
(1818). The remaining name, *Ceriantheomorphe brasiliensis*, is printed without an authority, and
both of its records point to the same accepted name, which the author confirmed.

The fuzzy candidates show why fuzzy matches are never accepted automatically. GBIF matched the
historical label *Palythoa caribbeorum* to *Palythoa caribbea*, a different species, with a
confidence of 0.98. The author rejected that match, while accepting WoRMS' phonetic match to
*P. caribaeorum*. That acceptance is supported by Table 3 of the article itself, which lists the
two names as the previous and the current identification of the same specimen.

All {{ r.overrides | words }} curated decisions ({{ r.overrides_accept | words }} accepted, {{ r.overrides_reject | words }}
rejected) are rows with evidence, and the pipeline fails if any of them is never used. Of the
names absent from NCBI Taxonomy, {{ r.ncbi.via_accepted | words }} could be linked through their WoRMS
accepted name. The remaining {{ r.ncbi.unresolved }} have no entry at all, which means no
sequence data for those taxa can be linked by name.

