### Knowledge graph, validation and queries

The graph contains {{ f.graph.triples | thousands }} triples about
{{ f.graph.subjects | thousands }} subjects:

- {{ f.graph.cls["dwc:Occurrence"] }} occurrences ({{ c.occurrence }} specimens and
  {{ c.distribution }} Table 1 assertions);
- {{ f.graph.cls["dwc:Identification"] }} identifications;
- {{ f.graph.cls["dwc:Taxon"] }} taxa ({{ r.n_taxa }} name usages from the article and
  {{ r.worms_records }} WoRMS records);
- {{ f.graph.cls["owl:Axiom"] }} SSSOM mapping records;
- {{ f.graph.cls["prov:Derivation"] }} qualified derivations, one for every record derived
  from the article.

It conforms to all {{ f.shacl.shapes }} shapes, with no violations. The
{{ f.shacl.warning | words }} warnings and the {{ f.shacl.info | words }} information result restate the gaps of the source.
To show that the shapes work, a copy of the graph with twelve independent faults injected was
validated in the tests, and each fault was reported on the right node.

{%- set p5 = f.q.q05 %}
Provenance can be queried end to end. For specimen MOHBR {{ p5[0].catalogNumber }}, one query
returns its origin in the article ({{ p5[0].sourceLocator }}, p. {{ p5[0].page }}). For each of
its {{ p5 | length | words }} identifier links, it also returns the service, the predicate, the
justification, the confidence, the request URL and the time of retrieval. All
{{ f.q.n_sparql | words }} SPARQL queries returned identical rows on RDFLib and on the Oxigraph
endpoint, and each SQL query returned the same rows as its SPARQL counterpart.

